>
  <p>School keeps you on track with bells, daily classes and teachers who notice when homework is missing. College removes all of that and hands you the freedom to fail quietly for six weeks. A planner is how you rebuild the scaffolding, on your own terms.</p>

  <h2 id="different">Why college is different</h2>
  <p>The meta-analyses by Richardson and by Credé and Kuncel find that study habits and self-regulation, not just ability, predict college performance. The challenge is that college spreads work thin: a reading here, a project due in a month, an exam that feels far away. Without a planner, the quiet weeks feel free, and then everything lands in the same fortnight.</p>

  <h2 id="setup">Set up your semester</h2>
  <p>In the first week, do one serious planning session:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Put every lecture and lab into the <a href="/tools/timetable-maker/">timetable maker</a>.</li>
    <li>Read each syllabus and enter every deadline, midterm and final date.</li>
    <li>Break big projects into dated steps now, not the week before, with the <a href="/tools/assignment-planner/">assignment planner</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For the full version, follow <a href="/guides/how-to-make-a-semester-study-plan/">how to make a semester study plan</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="weekly">The weekly habit</h2>
  <p>Every week, spend ten minutes reviewing the next two: what is due, what exams are coming, and which quiet work needs starting now. This is the self-regulation loop Zimmerman describes, applied to a term. It is the single habit that separates students who coast to finals calmly from those who panic.</p>

  <h2 id="tools">What to track</h2>
  <p>Track your grades as they come in with the <a href="/tools/gpa-calculator/">GPA calculator</a>, so you know which assignments and exams actually move your average and can aim effort accordingly. For managing the bigger assignments specifically, see <a href="/guides/how-to-manage-college-assignments/">how to manage college assignments and deadlines</a>.</p>
</Guide>
